////////////////////////////////////////////////////////////////////////////////////////////////////////////////////////////////
// This is the sample dialogue file - see DialogueFormat.md (same folder) for the full guide.
//
//

/*************************************************
Comments can be used
*/

// include("Name") references another dialogue file: keys that aren't found in this file are also
// searched there (and in whatever it includes). SampleShared.dialogue lives in this same folder.
// Run "Unity Common/Dialogue/Update References" after adding/renaming files - it re-links all
// includes and validates every jump in the project.
include("SampleShared")

// This defines a named dialogue (Tutorial in this case)
# Tutorial
// This one evaluates the expression inside the {} and goes to the first text that matches - this requires a implementation of the UCExpression.IContext interface in the same GameObject as the DialogueManager
{TutorialStage == 0}=>IntroText
{TutorialStage == 2}=>PresentSeed

# IntroText
{OneShot}
[TestSpeaker]: Welcome, Unlighted One!

I'm so happy you're here! Now, we can bring light back!

Darkness is very dangerous! Walk in the dark for a bit and you'll see!
// When this dialogue finishes, the code will be called. Code can be attributions (var = expression) or function calls (functionName(...))
=>{    
    TutorialStage = 1;
}

# Death1
[TestSpeaker]: See?!
Dangerous!!

But don't worry, you will always come back, although something may be lost in the process!
// When this text finishes, it will continue on the given text (in this case Death1:Question)
=>Death1:Question

# Death1:Question
[TestSpeaker]: Are you ready to go on?
// This represents a choice in the dialog. Each of the options lead to a different text element
*No, I'm scared! -> Death1:No
*Yes, let's go! -> Death1:Yes

# Death1:No
[TestSpeaker]: Don't be, as an Unlighted you always come back, even if it is unpleasant!
=>Death1:Question

# Death1:Yes
[TestSpeaker]: Yay! Very well! Follow the light and find the exit, I'll meet you in the other side!
=>{
    TutorialStage = 2;
    Teleport("Crystal", "CrystalTeleportLocation1");
}

# PresentSeed
[TestSpeaker]: You're here! I knew you could do it!

Now, grab that shovel over there, we're going to do something really neat!
=>{
    SpawnShovel();
}

// Weighted random branching: a run of consecutive {<weight>%}=> lines is a single group, rolled once.
// Weights are relative (they don't have to add up to 100) and a weight of 0 is never picked, so a
// branch can be switched off without deleting it. Plain conditions can be mixed in - they are still
// evaluated in order, so this reads as "if the tutorial is done always nag, otherwise roll":
# RandomGreeting
{TutorialStage == 0}=>IntroText
{50%}=>RandomGreeting:Hello
{30%}=>RandomGreeting:Weather
{20%}=>RandomGreeting:Silence

# RandomGreeting:Hello
[TestSpeaker]: Hello again!

# RandomGreeting:Weather
[TestSpeaker]: Still dark out there, isn't it?

# RandomGreeting:Silence
[TestSpeaker]: ...

// A code block no longer ends the dialogue: it runs and evaluation continues with the next line, so
// code and a redirect can be combined (this used to silently drop the =>RandomGreeting).
# RandomGreeting:Again
=>{
    TutorialStage = 2;
}
=>RandomGreeting

// Attributes: "@name=value" attaches arbitrary metadata to the element it is written in. The dialogue
// system doesn't interpret them at all - read them from the display or the context (HasAttribute,
// GetAttribute, GetAttributeNumber, GetAttributeBool on DialogueElement) and decide there what they
// mean for your game. A bare "@name" with no value reads as true, which is handy for flags.
// They can be written before or after the [Speaker] line of the element.
# AttributeSample
@portrait=old_man_worried
@shake=0.5
@skippable
[TestSpeaker]: The ground is moving!

// Text expressions: "${...}" anywhere in a line is evaluated against the context when the element is
// shown, and replaced by the result. The inside is a full expression, not just a variable name, so
// arithmetic works too. Set the variables from code (context.SetVariable) or from a code block before
// the text is reached. Something that can't be evaluated (a misspelt variable, say) is left exactly
// as written, so it's easy to spot in the game. Also works in option text and attribute values.
// (The bare "{ ... }" block is entry code - it runs on entering the node, before the text shows.)
# ExpressionSample
{
    sentCount = 5;
    survivorCount = 3;
}
[TestSpeaker]: Of the ${sentCount} who left, ${survivorCount} came back. We lost ${sentCount - survivorCount}.

// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
// STATE: one-shots, sticky rolls, local variables
//
// Everything a dialogue "remembers" lives in a DialogueState. The game can pass one per thing that
// starts a conversation (a point of interest, say) - then all of this is per-instance: two copies
// of the same event each roll their own rolls and spend their own one-shots. With no state passed,
// the global one is used and everything below still works, there's just a single memory.
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

// A question hub: {OneShot}* options can only be picked once each. {ShowOneShot} keeps the spent
// ones visible but greyed out (without it they just vanish). {ShowInvalid} does the same for options
// whose condition fails. A {GlobalOneShot}* option is spent across every instance of the event.
# HubSample
{ShowOneShot, ShowInvalid}
[TestSpeaker]: Ask me anything.
{OneShot}*Who are you? -> HubSample:Who
{OneShot}*What is this place? -> HubSample:Where
{OneShot && TutorialStage >= 2}*What was that seed about? -> HubSample:Seed
{GlobalOneShot}*Take the one coin she offers -> HubSample:Coin
*That's all -> HubSample:Done

# HubSample:Who
[TestSpeaker]: Nobody in particular.
=>HubSample

# HubSample:Where
[TestSpeaker]: The space between two better places.
=>HubSample

# HubSample:Seed
[TestSpeaker]: You'll see.
=>HubSample

# HubSample:Coin
[TestSpeaker]: There's only one, so make it count.
=>HubSample

# HubSample:Done
[TestSpeaker]: Safe travels.

// Entry code prefixed {OneShot}{ only runs the first time the node is entered - so this +1000 can
// be looped back to safely. A plain "{ ... }" block runs on every entry; a {GlobalOneShot}{ block
// runs once across every instance. Visits/HasSeen/HasRun (usable in any condition) read the same
// memory: Visits("Key") counts entries, HasSeen("Key") is Visits > 0, HasRun("Key") is whether the
// node's entry code ever executed.
# RewardSample
[TestSpeaker]: Here, take these supplies. (+1000 supplies)
{OneShot}{
    SupplyCount = 1000;
}
*Thanks -> RewardSample:After

# RewardSample:After
{HasSeen("HubSample")}=>RewardSample:KnownFace
[TestSpeaker]: Don't mention it.
=>RewardSample:Loop

# RewardSample:KnownFace
[TestSpeaker]: You again! Don't mention it.
=>RewardSample:Loop

// Coming back through here does NOT hand out another 1000 - the {OneShot}{ block is spent
# RewardSample:Loop
{Visits("RewardSample") < 3}=>RewardSample
=>HubSample:Done

// Weighted rolls are sticky by default: once this node has decided, it stays decided for that
// instance (a save file literally records which branch was taken). {AlwaysRoll} would make it roll
// fresh on every entry - and also applies to "{50%}*" option chances on the node.
# StickyRollSample
{25%}=>StickyRollSample:Rare
{75%}=>StickyRollSample:Common

# StickyRollSample:Rare
[TestSpeaker]: (You found the rare branch - this instance will always land here now.)

# StickyRollSample:Common
[TestSpeaker]: (The common branch - and this instance is stuck with it, which is usually what an
event wants. Add {AlwaysRoll} to the rolling node when it isn't.)

// "local." variables belong to the DialogueState of the conversation, so each instance of an event
// tracks its own - a plain variable is shared by the whole game. Tags on the node can also move the
// whole node's memory: {Global} makes everything stateful in it game-wide, {Local} (the default)
// keeps it per-instance.
# LocalVarSample
{
    local.timesGreeted = local.timesGreeted + 1;
}
[TestSpeaker]: Hello! I've greeted you ${local.timesGreeted} time(s) - though another copy of me
would start counting from zero.

// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
// MARKERS AND HISTORY
//
// {Marker=name} names a spot; "-> Marker(name)" jumps to it. Markers declared in the file the
// conversation STARTED in (and its includes) are known from the start, so a shared file (see
// SampleShared.dialogue) can hand control to whoever called it without knowing who that was - even
// "forward", to a node not visited yet. Visiting a marked node re-points the marker, which is what
// makes "go back to where we were" work. "-> History(-1)" instead goes back one step in what the
// player actually saw (History(0) restarts the current node); going back also rewinds the trail.
// Prefer markers for "return to a known place" - History(-n) with n > 1 depends on the path the
// player happened to take. "-> End" is the reserved way for an option to close the conversation.
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# MarkerSample
{Marker=return}
[TestSpeaker]: There's a stranger by the fire. Talk to them?
*Approach the stranger -> SampleShared:Stranger
*Peek at the previous page -> HistorySample
*Leave -> HubSample:Done

# HistorySample
[TestSpeaker]: Changed your mind?
*Yes, go back -> History(-1)
*Start this line over -> History(0)
*Actually, stop talking -> End
